          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em
              Poisson
              = succeed "-model" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            is a 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            whose target space is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+algebroid"} Poisson Lie algebroid
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-dimensional+Chern-Simons+theory"} 2-dimensional Chern-Simons theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string
            propagating on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            of a system in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
          %p
            In his solution of the problem of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
            showed that correlators for the 2-string interaction (the correlator on the worldsheet that is a disk with three marked points on its boundary) describe a product operation which is a deformation of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            on the target space. This solves the formal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            problem of the phase space in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            by identifying the quantum algebra with the
            %em open string algebra
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            on that target.
          %p The principal variant of the nonlinear Poisson sigma model is sometimes called Cattaneo-Felder model who have shown the graphical expansion used in Kontsevich’s approach to the deformation quantization is explained via a Feynman diagram expansion in this model.
          %p
            If one considers
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane"} brane
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-infinity+algebra"} A-infinity algebroid
          %blockquote
            %p
              Probably something close to a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+category"} Calabi-Yau category
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"} TCFT
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            of a Poisson
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \{\})
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+algebroid"} Poisson Lie algebroid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔓
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{P}
            corresponding to that.
          %p
            A field configuration on a 2-dimensional
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+an+infinity-bundle"} connection
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔗
                    %mo *
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝔓
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\phi,\eta) : \mathfrak{T}^*\Sigma \to \mathfrak{P}
                  \\,.
          %p In components this is
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo :
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo →
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : \Sigma \to X
            %li
              %p
                a 1-form
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi η
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo ,
                      %msup
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo *
                      %msup
                        %mi T
                        %mo *
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta \in \Omega^1(\Sigma, \phi^* T^* X)
                with values in the pullback of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cotangent+bundle"} cotangent bundle
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                along
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space"} configuration space
            of all such connections for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            is defined to be
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi Σ
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi η
                    %mo ∧
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi dR
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo +
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo *
                    %mi π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi η
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S : (\phi,\eta) \mapsto
                  \\int_\Sigma
                  \\left(
                  \\langle \eta \wedge d_{dR}\phi\rangle
                  +
                  \\frac{1}{2}
                  \\phi^*\pi(\eta)
                  \\right)
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mo ∈
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ∧
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi \in \wedge^2_{C^\infty(C)}\Gamma(T X)
            is the Poisson tensor of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \{-,-\})
            and where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle -,-\rangle
            is the canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+algebroid"} Poisson Lie algebroid
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#RelationToQuantizationOfPoissonManifolds Relation to deformation quantization of Poisson manifolds
          %p
            %a{:href => "#CattaneoFelder2000"} Cattaneo &amp; Felder 2000
            showed that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-point+function"} 3-point function
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral+quantization"} path integral quantization
            of the Poisson
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+algebroid"} Poisson Lie algebroid
            associated with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            computes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"} star product
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            of the Poisson manifold as given by
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Kontsevich"} Kontsevich
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometric+quantization"} higher geometric quantization
            that also yields the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+deformation+quantization"} strict deformation quantization
            is discussed at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+geometric+quantization+of+2d+Chern-Simons+theory"} extended geometric quantization of 2d Chern-Simons theory
          %p
            One may think of this relation between the 2d Poisson sigma-model and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            \= 1d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            as an example of the Chern-Simons type
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic principle
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#HolographicDual"} holographic dual
          %h3#Branes Branes
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branes"} branes
            of the Poisson sigma model are related to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coisotropic+submanifolds"} coisotropic submanifolds
            of the underlying
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+dg-submanifolds"} Lagrangian dg-submanifolds
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+algebroid"} Poisson Lie algebroid
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#CattaneoFelder03"} Cattaneo-Felder 03
          %h3#HolographicDual Holographic dual
          %p
            By the Chern-Simons form of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic principle
            one expects the Poisson sigma-model to be related to a 1-dimensional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            %a{:href => "#RelationToQuantizationOfPoissonManifolds"} above
            relation to the deformation quantization of Poisson manifolds goes in this direction. More explicit realizations have been attempted, for instance (
            %a{:href => "#Vassilevich"}> Vassilevich
            ).
